Z||000000||sjp777||05-12-2001||11:23 PM||||My do-it-yourself side scoop article is finally posted on my site. Sorry for the delay.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Nashco||05-12-2001||11:55 PM||||Well done sjp.  I haven't looked at my scoop yet, does it only have the two top screws?  This is a great project, in my opinion, because if you don't like it you're not out a bunch of cash.  After seeing how you got the smooth bend with the pvc elbow, I am thinking this would be really cool done with an aluminum mandrel bend.  Same idea, but sturdier because it could be TIG welded to the base, and more finishes available.  The only downfall is figuring out how to screw it in, if there's only two.  I'll have to take a look and see if a stud could be used and a nut put on the backside, or if it's too hard to reach from anywhere.  Personally, I love the look of satin and brushed aluminum, but there are some cars out there that would look good with polished aluminum.  Just a thought... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks for sharing your idea with us, I love the community atmosphere here!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||05-13-2001||12:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>Well done sjp.  I haven't looked at my scoop yet, does it only have the two top screws?  <P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, there are only 2 screws on the top. The bottom is held on by the 2 tabs that fit into slots in the body. Thanks for the positive feedback. If you manage to pull off the aluminum pipe alteration, let me know. That might work out even better. If you start making them to sell, I want a cut for the concept  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||05-13-2001||02:20 AM||||Hmm...interesting.  I'll probably pull my grate off tomorrow and check out mounting possiblities.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>If you start making them to sell, I want a cut for the concept<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cut?  You want a cut?  I'll give you a cut!  Where do you want it toughguy?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  Well, I don't know if I'd ever make something to sell, but if I can find the resources, I'd make ya one for cost.  That's a ways down the road though, as I'm in the middle of about ten other projects right now (literally!).  So, did you just use spray paint for yours?  The pics from afar look great, but what kind of up close quality is there with the pvc?  Any funky warps or imperfections with the paint?  I'm not dissing anything by any means, just curious.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||05-13-2001||12:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>  So, did you just use spray paint for yours?  The pics from afar look great, but what kind of up close quality is there with the pvc?  Any funky warps or imperfections with the paint?  I'm not dissing anything by any means, just curious.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Paint is perfect. No imperfections. I wet sanded the pvc for hours before I ever started to paint. Used duplicolor GM black and clear coat in cans. Multiple coats of each. Spray can paint tends to get a bit of orange peel texture, so to get rid of that you need to do a final wet sand with 1000 grit or better paper after the final clear coat has had about a week to dry. Polishing compound will bring the gloss back after you sand. Wax seals the deal. Paint on my scoop is actually smoother than the factory body paint.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||TOM||05-13-2001||03:26 PM||||That looks really nice. I like the smooothness of it. How much in time is envolved. Here is my scoop. <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=141908&a=1039804&p=47928779&Sequence=0&res=high" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=141908&a=1039804&p=47928779&Sequence=0&res=high</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||05-13-2001||04:17 PM||||If you go to his web page and check out his tutorial, all the details are there.  As he just explained his painting method, it's hard to say how long it will take.  You could have it done in an afternoon, or you could make it take two weeks, depending on what you want to do with it.  If you weren't picky about the paint, and just wanted the scoop, you could easily have it done in two hours (plus epoxy drying time) if you have the necessary tools.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
